 \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)” yields the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  All lines determined by points of \\(\\mathcal P\\)\n\nFor each unordered pair of points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) compute the slope.  \nThe distinct lines obtained are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&x=1,\\;x=2,\\;y=1,\\;y=2,\\\\\n&x+y=3,\\;x+y=4,\\\\\n&y=x,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\;y=-2x+5 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe first six lines have slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and are therefore **non‑sunny**.  \nThe last three lines have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) and are **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Points on the sunny lines\n\nA direct check shows\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&:y=x &&\\text{contains }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\nL_{2}&:y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52 &&\\text{contains }(1,2),(3,1),\\\\\nL_{3}&:y=-2x+5 &&\\text{contains }(1,3),(2,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nNo other point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on any of these three lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Impossibility of exactly two sunny lines\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exist three distinct lines\n\\(L_{a},L_{b},L_{c}\\) covering \\(\\mathcal P\\) such that exactly two of them are sunny.\nSince any sunny line must be one of \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) (the only sunny lines that pass through at least two points of \\(\\mathcal P\\)), the two sunny lines must be a subset of \\(\\{L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\}\\).\n\nConsequently the two sunny lines together cover at most four points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \nThe two points not covered are precisely the pair belonging to the omitted sunny line:\n\n* If the sunny lines are \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\), the uncovered points are \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\), which lie on \\(L_{3}\\).\n* If the sunny lines are \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{3}\\), the uncovered points are \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\), which lie on \\(L_{2}\\).\n* If the sunny lines are \\(L_{2}\\) and \\(L_{3}\\), the uncovered points are \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\), which lie on \\(L_{1}\\).\n\nThus in every case the two uncovered points are collinear on a **sunny** line.\n\nA non‑sunny line (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or to \\(x+y=0\\)) that passes through two points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) must be one of the six lines listed in (1). None of those six lines contains any of the three pairs above. Hence the third line, which must be non‑sunny, cannot pass through both uncovered points.\n\nThe only remaining possibility would be that the non‑sunny line passes through exactly one of the two uncovered points. The other point would then remain uncovered, contradicting the covering requirement.\n\nTherefore a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Existence of the other values of \\(k\\)\n\nFor completeness we note explicit constructions for the attainable values of \\(k\\):\n\n* \\(k=0\\): use the three non‑sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=4\\).  \n  Each of the six points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n* \\(k=1\\): use the sunny line \\(y=x\\) together with the non‑sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  \n  All points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) are covered.\n\n* \\(k=3\\): use the three sunny lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) from (2).  \n  Each of the six points belongs to exactly one of these lines.\n\nThus the only possible numbers of sunny lines for \\(n=3\\) are \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\); the value \\(k=2\\) is impossible. ∎"
