at only the slopes \\(1\\) and \\(-2\\) can contain \\(t\\) points of \\(T_k\\); each of these slopes yields **exactly one** line with \\(t\\) points (the main diagonal \\(y=x\\) and the line \\(y=-2x+k+2\\)).  Hence at most two sunny lines can have the required size \\(t\\), but we need \\(k\\ge5\\) such lines \u2013 a contradiction.  This is *Lemma\u202f4*.\n\n6. **Existence for \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=3\\).**  \n   *Lemma\u202f5* gives explicit constructions:\n\n   - \\(k=1\\): the single sunny line \\(y=x\\) covers the unique point \\((1,1)\\) of \\(T_1\\).\n   - \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     L_1 &: y=-2x+5,\\\\\n     L_2 &: x+2y=5,\\\\\n     L_3 &: y=x,\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n\n     cover all six points of \\(T_3\\).  A point\u2011by\u2011point verification is provided in the detailed proof.\n\n7. **Lifting to arbitrary \\(n\\).**  \n   Using Lemma\u202f1, the coverings of \\(T_1\\) and \\(T_3\\) extend to any \\(n\\ge3\\) by adding \\(n-1\\) (resp. \\(n-3\\)) vertical lines.  The case \\(k=0\\) is obtained by taking the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only admissible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0,1,3\\).  Hence the final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\n#### 1.  The set of points to be covered  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically the points of \\(T_n\\) form a right\u2011triangle with vertices\n\\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  Its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  Non\u2011sunny\nlines are therefore vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.  Reduction to a self\u2011covering problem  \n\n**Lemma\u202f1.**  \nLet \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  There exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of\nthem sunny, covering all points of \\(T_n\\) **iff** the triangle \\(T_k\\) can\nbe covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\)\nsunny lines covers \\(T_n\\).  Among the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines we may\nreplace each of them by a vertical line \\(x=c\\) without losing any\ncovered point: a vertical line can cover at least as many points of a\ncolumn as any other non\u2011sunny line, and the set of columns covered by the\n\\(n-k\\) vertical lines can be chosen to be \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\).  After\nthis replacement the points of columns \\(1,\\dots ,n-k\\) are covered, and\nthe uncovered points are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich, after the change of variables \\(a' = a-(n-k)\\), is exactly\n\\(T_k\\).  Hence the remaining \\(k\\) sunny lines cover \\(T_k\\).\n\nConversely, if \\(T_k\\) is covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines, add the\n\\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  These vertical lines cover\nall points of \\(T_n\\) except those of the translated triangle\n\\(T_k\\); the sunny lines then cover the remainder.  The total number of\nlines is \\(n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny. \u220e\n\nThus the original problem is equivalent to the following one:\n\n> **Problem\u202fR.**  For which non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) does there exist a\n> covering of the triangle \\(T_k\\) by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines?\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  A bound on the number of points a sunny line can contain  \n\nLet a sunny line \\(L\\) intersect \\(T_k\\) in at least two points.\nWrite its slope in lowest terms as \\(m=p/q\\) with \\(q>0\\) and\n\\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\).  The line has equation  \n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{p}{q}x + c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb Q).\n\\]\n\nIf \\((x_1,y_1)\\) and \\((x_2,y_2)\\) are two integer points of \\(L\\),\nthen \\(x_2-x_1\\) is a multiple of \\(q\\); consequently the set of\n\\(x\\)-coordinates of integer points on \\(L\\) is an arithmetic progression\nwith difference \\(q\\).\n\nLet \\(t\\) be the number of points of \\(T_k\\) lying on \\(L\\).  The smallest\nand largest \\(x\\)-coordinates among those points differ by at least\n\\((t-1)q\\).  Since all points of \\(T_k\\) have \\(1\\le x\\le k\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(t-1)q\\le k-1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad q\\le\\frac{k-1}{t-1}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe same argument applied to the \\(y\\)-coordinates (using the direction\nvector \\((q,p)\\)) yields \\(|p|\\le\\frac{k-1}{t-1}\\).\n\nIn the covering problem we shall have \\(t\\ge\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) (otherwise\nthe total number of points covered by the \\(k\\) lines would be\n\\(<k\\cdot\\frac{k+1}{2}=|T_k|\\)).  Substituting \\(t-1\\ge\\frac{k-1}{2}\\)\ninto (2) gives  \n\n\\[\nq\\le2,\\qquad |p|\\le2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence the reduced slope \\(p/q\\) can be only one of\n\n\\[\n1,\\;2,\\;-2,\\;\\frac12,\\;-\\frac12 .\n\\]\n\nMoreover, a line of slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) is excluded because\nit would be non\u2011sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Even values of \\(k\\) are impossible  \n\n**Lemma\u202f2.**  If \\(k>0\\) is even, no covering of \\(T_k\\) by \\(k\\) sunny\nlines exists.\n\n*Proof.*  By Lemma\u202f3 (proved below) each sunny line contains at most\n\\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_k\\).  Since\n\\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor=\\frac{k}{2}\\) for even \\(k\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{total points covered}\\le k\\cdot\\frac{k}{2}= \\frac{k^2}{2}\n   <\\frac{k(k+1)}2=|T_k|,\n\\]\n\ncontradicting the requirement that every point of \\(T_k\\) be covered. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  Odd values \\(k\\ge5\\) are impossible  \n\nFrom now on assume \\(k\\) is odd and \\(k\\ge5\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\nt=\\frac{k+1}{2}\\qquad(\\text{an integer}).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|T_k|=k t\\), a covering by \\(k\\) lines forces **every** line\nto contain exactly \\(t\\) points of \\(T_k\\); otherwise the total number of\ncovered points would be \\(<k t\\).\n\nBy (3) the only possible slopes are \\(1,2,-2,\\frac12,-\\frac12\\).  A\ndirect inspection (using the description of the points of \\(T_k\\)) shows\nthe following:\n\n* a line of slope \\(1\\) has the form \\(y=x+c\\).  It contains \\(t\\) points\n  of \\(T_k\\) **iff** \\(c=0\\); then the points are \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots,\n  (t,t)\\).\n\n* a line of slope \\(-2\\) has the form \\(y=-2x+c\\).  It contains \\(t\\)\n  points of \\(T_k\\) **iff** \\(c=k+2\\); then the points are\n  \\((1,k),(2,k-2),\\dots,(t,1)\\).\n\n* a line of slope \\(2\\) (equation \\(y=2x+c\\)), of slope \\(\\frac12\\)\n  (\\(y=\\tfrac12x+c\\)), or of slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) (\\(y=-\\tfrac12x+c\\))\n  can contain at most \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{3}\\big\\rfloor<t\\) points of\n  \\(T_k\\) (the inequalities follow from the constraints\n  \\(x+y\\le k+1\\) and \\(x\\ge1\\)).  Hence such slopes cannot appear in a\n  covering.\n\nConsequently a line that contains the required \\(t\\) points must have\nslope either \\(1\\) or \\(-2\\).  As shown above there is **exactly one**\nline of each of these two slopes that contains \\(t\\) points.  Therefore\nthe triangle \\(T_k\\) can be covered by at most two sunny lines, while a\ncovering would need \\(k\\ge5\\) lines \u2013 a contradiction.\n\nThus no covering exists for any odd \\(k\\ge5\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 6.  Existence for \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=3\\)\n\n**Lemma\u202f3.**  The triangles \\(T_1\\) and \\(T_3\\) admit coverings by\n\\(1\\) and \\(3\\) sunny lines, respectively.\n\n*Proof for \\(k=1\\).*  The single point of \\(T_1\\) is \\((1,1)\\).  The line\n\\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) passes through it and is sunny.  Hence a covering\nby one sunny line exists.\n\n*Proof for \\(k=3\\).*  The six points of \\(T_3\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nConsider the three lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = -2x+5,\\\\\nL_2 &: x+2y = 5,\\\\\nL_3 &: y = x .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll three slopes (\\(-2,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\,1\\)) are different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence the lines are sunny and distinct.\n\nVerification point\u2011by\u2011point:\n\n* \\(L_1\\):  \\(y=-2x+5\\) gives \\(5-2\\cdot1=3\\) and \\(5-2\\cdot2=1\\); thus\n  \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) lie on \\(L_1\\).\n\n* \\(L_2\\):  \\(x+2y=5\\) gives \\(1+2\\cdot2=5\\) and \\(3+2\\cdot1=5\\); thus\n  \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) lie on \\(L_2\\).\n\n* \\(L_3\\):  \\(y=x\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\).\n\nThese six points are exactly the points of \\(T_3\\), each belonging to\nexactly one of the three lines.  Hence \\(T_3\\) is covered by three sunny\nlines. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 7.  Lifting the coverings to arbitrary \\(n\\)\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots,\\;x=n .\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so the point lies\non the line \\(x=a\\).  All lines are non\u2011sunny, giving \\(k=0\\).\n\n*Case \\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\)\nand the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; y-1 = \\frac{1}{1-n}(x-n) \\qquad\\bigl(\\text{slope } -\\tfrac1{\\,n-1}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\n\\(L\\) passes through the unique uncovered point \\((n,1)\\); its slope is\nneither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\) (because \\(n\\ge3\\)), so \\(L\\) is\nsunny.  Every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered either by a vertical line\n(\\(a\\le n-1\\)) or by \\(L\\) (\\(a=n\\)).  Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n*Case \\(k=3\\).*  Apply Lemma\u202f1 with the covering of \\(T_3\\) from Lemma\u202f3.\nTake the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-3\n\\]\n\nand translate the three lines of Lemma\u202f3 by the vector \\((n-3,0)\\).  The\ntranslated lines are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = -2x + (2n-1),\\\\\nL_2 &: x + 2y = n+2,\\\\\nL_3 &: y = x - n + 3 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll three slopes (\\(-2,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\,1\\)) are sunny.  The points of the\nremaining triangle \\(\\{(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\}\\)\nare covered as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point} & \\text{covering line}\\\\ \\hline\n(n-2,3) & L_1\\\\\n(n-1,1) & L_1\\\\\n(n-2,2) & L_2\\\\\n(n,1)   & L_2\\\\\n(n-2,1) & L_3\\\\\n(n-1,2) & L_3\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, and exactly three of the lines\nare sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 8.  Final answer  \n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 the existence of a configuration for a given pair \\((n,k)\\)\nis equivalent to the existence of a covering of \\(T_k\\) by \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.  Lemmas\u202f2\u20135 show that such a covering exists precisely for\n\\(k=0,1,3\\).  Consequently, for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of\nadmissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}} .\n\\]"
